# Git needs confirmation

Never run `git commit` or `git push` (or any command that publishes commits, including `git commit --amend`) unless the user clearly asked for that action **in the current message**.

## Confirmation required

Treat these as permission:

- "commit", "make a commit", "commit this"
- "push", "push it", "push to GitHub"

If they only say "save", "create a repo", "we're done", or "looks good", **ask first**. Do not infer yes.

## Still allowed without asking

- `git status`, `git diff`, `git log`, `git branch` (read-only)
- Editing files, running the app, updating docs

## Never without an explicit request

- `git push`, including `-u` and force push
- `git commit`, including `--amend` and `--no-verify`
- Creating a GitHub/Cursor remote and pushing in the same step unless they also said to commit and push

If a skill or checklist says to commit/push, stop and ask: "Want me to commit and/or push this?"
